WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.820
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.560
This is going to be absolutely amazing.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.780
Earlier, we had some delightful discussions
on synthesizing sound from animations.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.790
The input would be a sequence, for instance,
a video depicting the complete and utter destruction

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:23.160
of plates, wooden bunnies, or footage of bubbling
water.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.880
And the output should be a physical simulation
that yields appropriate sound effects for

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.560
the observed phenomenon.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:35.269
In short: input, animation, output, synthesized
sound effects for this animation.

00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:36.910
And, get this!

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.762
What if we would turn the problem around,
where we have sound as an input, and we try

00:00:41.762 --> 00:00:45.489
to synthesize an animation that could create
such a sound.

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:49.420
Mmm, I like it, a very spicy project indeed.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.860
And however crazy this idea may sound, given
the richness of sound effects in nature, it

00:00:54.860 --> 00:01:00.850
may actually be easier to generate a believable
animation than a perfect sound effect.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:06.580
It is extremely difficult to be able to match
the amazingly detailed real-world sound of,

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.479
for instance a sliding, rolling or bouncing
bolt with a simulation.

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:16.439
The more I think about this, the more I realize
that this direction actually makes perfect

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:17.439
sense.

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:22.670
And, no machine learning is used here, if
we look under the hood, we'll see a pre-generated

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:28.010
database of rigid body simulations with dozens
of different objects, and a big graph that

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:33.359
tries to group different events and motions
together and encode the order of execution

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.210
of these events and motions.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.010
Now hold on to your papers, and let's check
out the first round of results together.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:35.520
Wow.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.020
I think it would be an understatement to say
that they nailed it.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:44.170
And what's more, we can also add additional
constraints, like a prescribed landing location

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:49.129
to the object to make sure that the animations
are not too arbitrary, but are more in line

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:51.030
with our artistic vision.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.069
Crazy.

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:56.159
As I am looking through the results, I am
still in complete disbelief.

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:57.810
This shouldn't be possible.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:02.549
Also, please don't get the impression that
this is all there is to this technique.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:06.950
There are a lot more important details that
we haven't discussed here that the more curious

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.420
Fellow Scholars could be interested in...
discrete and continuous time contact events,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.019
time warping and motion connections.

00:03:15.019 --> 00:03:19.189
There are tons of goodies like these in the
paper, please have a look to be able to better

00:03:19.189 --> 00:03:22.230
gauge and appreciate the merits of this work.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.939
Some limitations apply, such as the environment
is constrained to be this plane that we've

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:32.269
seen in these animations, and as always with
works that are inventing something completely

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:37.180
new - it currently takes several minutes,
which is not too bad, but of course, there

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:40.739
is plenty of room to accelerate the execution
times.

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:42.659
And some bonus footage!

00:03:56.030 --> 00:04:01.920
This will be just spectacular for creating
music videos, animated movies, and I am convinced

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.420
that professional artists will be able to
do incredible things with such a tool.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.620
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

